      "role": "user",
      "content": "hi, i'm studying phonetics and i'm a bit confused. can you explain the difference between voiced and unvoiced alveolar fricatives? specifically the difference between /s/ and /z/ sounds in terms of what the vocal cords are doing?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Of course! Voiced sounds, like /z/, involve the vibration of the vocal cords, while unvoiced sounds, like /s/, do not. So, when you produce the /z/ sound ('zebra'), your vocal cords vibrate, and the air flows over them, producing a buzzing sound. For the /s/ sound ('sugar'), your vocal cords do not vibrate, and only the air flow over the ridge of your tongue next to your teeth creates the hissing sound.",
